    <p class="mh">Work with us</p><a class="mt" href="/careers"><b>Careers</b><small>Clinical education sales for RNs and LPNs</small></a>
    <p class="mh">Account</p><a class="mt" href="/#signup"><b>Join with a school code</b><small>Or request access</small></a><a class="mt" href="/#signin"><b>Sign in</b><small>Already have one</small></a>
   </div></details></nav></div></header><script>(function(){var d=document.querySelector(".mnav");if(!d)return;document.addEventListener("click",function(e){if(d.open&&!d.contains(e.target))d.open=false});document.addEventListener("keydown",function(e){if(e.key==="Escape"&&d.open){d.open=false;d.querySelector("summary").focus()}})})();</script><main><nav class="crumb w" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> › <a href="/nclex-practice-questions">Practice questions</a> › <span>Psychosocial Integrity</span></nav><section class="hero w"><h1>Psychosocial Integrity NCLEX Practice Questions</h1><p class="lead">Mental health, coping, crisis, abuse, and therapeutic communication. Try 8 free questions below, each with the answer, a rationale, and the source it was checked against.</p>
      <p><span class="pill">6–12% of the NCLEX-RN</span><span class="pill">123 questions in Vexcura</span><span class="pill">Reviewed November 2026</span></p><div class="cta"><a class="btn" href="/#practice/PSY">Practice more Psychosocial Integrity questions</a><a class="btn alt" href="/free-nclex-practice-exam">Take the full practice exam</a></div></section>
      <div class="w"><h2>What this category covers</h2><p>Psychosocial Integrity is one of the eight client needs categories in the NCSBN NCLEX-RN test plan. Expect questions on Therapeutic communication, Suicide risk, Substance use, Grief, Psychosis, and Abuse and neglect.</p>
      <div class="tips"><b>How to study Psychosocial Integrity</b><ul><li>Practice therapeutic communication: pick the response that explores feelings, reflects, or stays with the client; reject 'why' questions, false reassurance, and advice.</li><li>Safety first in psych: any hint of suicide gets a direct question about intent and a plan.</li><li>Review withdrawal timelines and signs for alcohol, opioids, and benzodiazepines.</li><li>Know defense mechanisms, grief stages, and how delirium differs from dementia and depression.</li></ul></div>
      <h2>Free Psychosocial Integrity practice questions</h2><article class="q" data-t="mc" id="psy4-018">
     <div class="meta">Psychosocial Integrity · Multiple choice · Take action</div>
     <p class="stem">A client with schizophrenia stands in the corner staring and doesn't respond to questions. What should the nurse do?</p><ol><li data-c="1"><label><input type="radio" name="q-psy4-018"> <span><b>A.</b> Stay with the client, speak simply, and check physical needs</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-psy4-018"> <span><b>B.</b> Tell the client to stop acting strange</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-psy4-018"> <span><b>C.</b> Leave the client alone until they talk</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-psy4-018"> <span><b>D.</b> Demand an answer</span></label></li></ol>
     <button class="btn chk" type="button">Check answer</button><p class="res" aria-live="polite"></p>
     <details><summary>Show answer and rationale</summary><p class="ans">Correct answer: A</p><p>For a withdrawn or catatonic client, the nurse offers presence, uses short simple statements, and makes sure basic needs like nutrition, hydration, and toileting are met.</p><p class="src">Source: Varcarolis' Foundations of Psychiatric-Mental Health Nursing, 10th ed.</p></details>
     <p class="more"><a href="/nclex-questions/schizophrenia-stands-corner-staring-doesn-t-respond-psy4-018">Open this question on its own page</a></p></article><article class="q" data-t="mc" id="psy4-021">
     <div class="meta">Psychosocial Integrity · Multiple choice · Generate solutions</div>
     <p class="stem">An older client lives alone, has lost weight, and the home is cluttered with spoiled food. The client is alert and oriented. What should the nurse do?</p><ol><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-psy4-021"> <span><b>A.</b> Ignore it because the client is competent</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-psy4-021"> <span><b>B.</b> Force the client into a nursing home</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-psy4-021"> <span><b>C.</b> Clean the house without asking</span></label></li><li data-c="1"><label><input type="radio" name="q-psy4-021"> <span><b>D.</b> Assess the client's ability to care for self and refer to adult protective services or social services for possible self-neglect</span></label></li></ol>
     <button class="btn chk" type="button">Check answer</button><p class="res" aria-live="polite"></p>
     <details><summary>Show answer and rationale</summary><p class="ans">Correct answer: D</p><p>Self-neglect is a form of elder mistreatment that can be reported. The nurse assesses safety and connects the client with resources while respecting autonomy.</p><p class="src">Source: National Center on Elder Abuse; state adult protective services statutes</p></details>
     <p class="more"><a href="/nclex-questions/older-lives-alone-lost-weight-home-cluttered-psy4-021">Open this question on its own page</a></p></article><article class="q" data-t="mc" id="psy4-019">
     <div class="meta">Psychosocial Integrity · Multiple choice · Analyze cues</div>
     <p class="stem">A client describes feeling detached from their body, as if watching themselves from outside. Which symptom is this?</p><ol><li data-c="1"><label><input type="radio" name="q-psy4-019"> <span><b>A.</b> Depersonalization</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-psy4-019"> <span><b>B.</b> Delusion of grandeur</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-psy4-019"> <span><b>C.</b> Hallucination</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-psy4-019"> <span><b>D.</b> Flight of ideas</span></label></li></ol>
     <button class="btn chk" type="button">Check answer</button><p class="res" aria-live="polite"></p>
     <details><summary>Show answer and rationale</summary><p class="ans">Correct answer: A</p><p>Depersonalization is a sense of detachment from one's body or thoughts. It can occur with severe anxiety, trauma, or dissociative disorders.</p><p class="src">Source: APA. DSM-5-TR (dissociative symptoms)</p></details>
     <p class="more"><a href="/nclex-questions/describes-feeling-detached-body-if-watching-themselves-psy4-019">Open this question on its own page</a></p></article><article class="q" data-t="sata" id="psy4-016">
     <div class="meta">Psychosocial Integrity · Select all that apply · Take action</div>
     <p class="stem">Which communication techniques are therapeutic? Select all that apply.</p><ol><li data-c="1"><label><input type="checkbox" name="q-psy4-016"> <span><b>A.</b> Offering self, such as &quot;I'll stay with you&quot;</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="checkbox" name="q-psy4-016"> <span><b>B.</b> Asking &quot;why&quot; questions to understand behavior</span></label></li><li data-c="1"><label><input type="checkbox" name="q-psy4-016"> <span><b>C.</b> Restating the client's message</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="checkbox" name="q-psy4-016"> <span><b>D.</b> Giving approval, such as &quot;That's the right thing to do&quot;</span></label></li><li data-c="1"><label><input type="checkbox" name="q-psy4-016"> <span><b>E.</b> Using open-ended questions</span></label></li></ol>
     <button class="btn chk" type="button">Check answer</button><p class="res" aria-live="polite"></p>
     <details><summary>Show answer and rationale</summary><p class="ans">Correct answers: A, C, E</p><p>Open-ended questions, restating, and offering self encourage communication. Giving approval or disapproval and &quot;why&quot; questions can block communication or put clients on the defensive.</p><p class="src">Source: Varcarolis' Foundations of Psychiatric-Mental Health Nursing, 10th ed.</p></details>
     <p class="more"><a href="/nclex-questions/communication-techniques-therapeutic-select-all-apply-psy4-016">Open this question on its own page</a></p></article><article class="q" data-t="mc" id="psy4-020">
     <div class="meta">Psychosocial Integrity · Multiple choice · Generate solutions</div>
     <p class="stem">A client has trouble coping after a job loss. Which action best supports healthy coping?</p><ol><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-psy4-020"> <span><b>A.</b> Tell the client not to worry</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-psy4-020"> <span><b>B.</b> Tell the client to avoid talking about it</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-psy4-020"> <span><b>C.</b> Encourage drinking alcohol to relax</span></label></li><li data-c="1"><label><input type="radio" name="q-psy4-020"> <span><b>D.</b> Help the client identify supports and problem-solving steps</span></label></li></ol>
     <button class="btn chk" type="button">Check answer</button><p class="res" aria-live="polite"></p>
     <details><summary>Show answer and rationale</summary><p class="ans">Correct answer: D</p><p>Supporting problem-solving, identifying strengths and supports, and exploring feelings promote adaptive coping.</p><p class="src">Source: Varcarolis' Foundations of Psychiatric-Mental Health Nursing, 10th ed.</p></details>
     <p class="more"><a href="/nclex-questions/trouble-coping-job-loss-action-supports-healthy-psy4-020">Open this question on its own page</a></p></article><article class="q" data-t="mc" id="psy4-009">
     <div class="meta">Psychosocial Integrity · Multiple choice · Recognize cues</div>
     <p class="stem">Which finding in a young child may indicate sexual abuse?</p><ol><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-psy4-009"> <span><b>A.</b> A scraped knee from a fall</span></label></li><li data-c="1"><label><input type="radio" name="q-psy4-009"> <span><b>B.</b> Age-inappropriate sexual knowledge or behavior</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-psy4-009"> <span><b>C.</b> Fear of the dark</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-psy4-009"> <span><b>D.</b> Interest in playing doctor with a toy kit</span></label></li></ol>
     <button class="btn chk" type="button">Check answer</button><p class="res" aria-live="polite"></p>
     <details><summary>Show answer and rationale</summary><p class="ans">Correct answer: B</p><p>Sexual knowledge or behavior beyond the child's developmental level, genital injuries, and sudden behavior changes are concerning signs that warrant further evaluation and reporting.</p><p class="src">Source: AAP. Clinical Report: The Evaluation of Children in the Primary Care Setting When Sexual Abuse Is Suspected</p></details>
     <p class="more"><a href="/nclex-questions/finding-young-child-may-indicate-sexual-abuse-psy4-009">Open this question on its own page</a></p></article><article class="q" data-t="mc" id="psy4-012">
     <div class="meta">Psychosocial Integrity · Multiple choice · Analyze cues</div>
     <p class="stem">How do most school-age children (about 9 to 10 years) understand death?</p><ol><li data-c="1"><label><input type="radio" name="q-psy4-012"> <span><b>A.</b> Death is permanent and happens to everyone</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-psy4-012"> <span><b>B.</b> Death is temporary like sleep</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-psy4-012"> <span><b>C.</b> Death only happens to bad people</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-psy4-012"> <span><b>D.</b> They have no concept of death</span></label></li></ol>
     <button class="btn chk" type="button">Check answer</button><p class="res" aria-live="polite"></p>
     <details><summary>Show answer and rationale</summary><p class="ans">Correct answer: A</p><p>By about age 9 or 10, most children understand that death is final, universal, and has causes.</p><p class="src">Source: Hockenberry. Wong's Nursing Care of Infants and Children, 12th ed.</p></details>
     <p class="more"><a href="/nclex-questions/school-age-children-9-10-years-understand-death-psy4-012">Open this question on its own page</a></p></article><article class="q" data-t="mc" id="psy4-013">
     <div class="meta">Psychosocial Integrity · Multiple choice · Take action</div>
     <p class="stem">A client recovering from a myocardial infarction asks when it's safe to resume sexual activity. Which response reflects current guidance?</p><ol><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-psy4-013"> <span><b>A.</b> You can resume as soon as you get home</span></label></li><li data-c="1"><label><input type="radio" name="q-psy4-013"> <span><b>B.</b> It's generally reasonable once you can do mild to moderate activity, like climbing two flights of stairs, without symptoms</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-psy4-013"> <span><b>C.</b> You should never have sex again</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-psy4-013"> <span><b>D.</b> Ask your family instead</span></label></li></ol>
     <button class="btn chk" type="button">Check answer</button><p class="res" aria-live="polite"></p>
     <details><summary>Show answer and rationale</summary><p class="ans">Correct answer: B</p><p>For uncomplicated MI, sexual activity is often reasonable about a week or more later if the client can do mild to moderate activity (3 to 5 METs) without symptoms. The nurse addresses sexuality openly as part of care.</p><p class="src">Source: AHA. Scientific Statement: Sexual Activity and Cardiovascular Disease (2012)</p></details>
     <p class="more"><a href="/nclex-questions/recovering-myocardial-infarction-asks-s-safe-resume-psy4-013">Open this question on its own page</a></p></article>
      <div class="cta"><a class="btn" href="/#practice/PSY">Practice more Psychosocial Integrity questions</a><a class="btn alt" href="/free-nclex-practice-exam">Take the full practice exam</a></div><div class="split rev"><div class="st"><h2>See your Psychosocial Integrity score after a full exam</h2><p>The full adaptive practice exam runs 85 to 150 items on a five-hour clock and stops by the same rules as the NCLEX. Results break down by all 8 client needs categories, with a pass-likelihood estimate, so students know exactly what to study next. It's free for students through a licensed nursing school, and every plan includes everything.</p><p><a href="/free-nclex-practice-exam">The full practice exam</a></p></div><figure class="shot"><div class="dv dv-b"><div class="dv-bar" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span><svg viewBox="0 0 12 14" width="9" height="11" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 6V4.2a3 3 0 0 1 6 0V6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"/><rect x="1.5" y="6" width="9" height="7" rx="1.6" fill="currentColor"/></svg>nclexsim.com</span></div><div class="dv-v"><img src="/img/exam-results-cats-1000.webp" srcset="/img/exam-results-cats-1600.webp 1600w, /img/exam-results-cats-1000.webp 1000w" sizes="(max-width: 760px) calc(100vw - 32px), 720px" width="1000" height="625" alt="Full practice exam results by NCLEX client needs category, from below to above the passing standard" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div></div><figcaption>Full-exam results by client needs category. Demo student.</figcaption></figure></div><p style="font-size:15.5px;color:var(--muted)">Took an outside NCLEX-readiness test at school? <a href="/ati-score-report-study-plan">Turn your score report into a study plan</a> built from the topics it lists.</p><h2>Other NCLEX categories</h2><div class="grid"><a class="card" href="/nclex-practice-questions/management-of-care"><b>Management of Care</b><small>15–21% of the exam</small></a><a class="card" href="/nclex-practice-questions/safety-and-infection-control"><b>Safety and Infection Prevention and Control</b><small>10–16% of the exam</small></a><a class="card" href="/nclex-practice-questions/health-promotion-and-maintenance"><b>Health Promotion and Maintenance</b><small>6–12% of the exam</small></a><a class="card" href="/nclex-practice-questions/basic-care-and-comfort"><b>Basic Care and Comfort</b><small>6–12% of the exam</small></a><a class="card" href="/nclex-practice-questions/pharmacology"><b>Pharmacological and Parenteral Therapies</b><small>13–19% of the exam</small></a><a class="card" href="/nclex-practice-questions/reduction-of-risk-potential"><b>Reduction of Risk Potential</b><small>9–15% of the exam</small></a><a class="card" href="/nclex-practice-questions/physiological-adaptation"><b>Physiological Adaptation</b><small>11–17% of the exam</small></a></div><h2>Related</h2><ul class="rel"><li><a href="/free-nclex-practice-exam">Full adaptive NCLEX-RN practice exam</a></li><li><a href="/nclex-prioritization-questions">NCLEX prioritization questions</a></li><li><a href="/next-generation-nclex">Next Generation NCLEX (NGN) guide</a></li><li><a href="/nclex-study-guides">Free NCLEX study guides (PDF)</a></li><li><a href="/nclex-pass-guarantee">NCLEX pass guarantee: registration fee back if you qualify and don't pass</a></li></ul></div><section class="band vxs-band"><div class="vxs-bg" data-vxs-i="0" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="vxs-sh" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="w"><h2>Bring Vexcura to your nursing program</h2><p>1,220 cited NCLEX-RN questions, adaptive practice exams, 8 NGN case studies, an Epic-style academic EHR and a clinical instructor workspace. The school pays; students and instructors are always free, and every plan includes everything. Students: ask your instructor, or join with your school's code.</p><div class="cta"><a class="btn" href="/for-nursing-schools">See Vexcura for nursing schools</a><a class="btn alt" href="/for-nursing-schools#pricing">Pricing</a><a class="btn alt" href="/#signup">Join with a school code</a></div></div></section></main><footer class="foot"><div class="w"><div class="cols">
